  "img": "s18_how-many-bartenders-do-i-need",
  "alt": "Guests crowd a long event bar while a bartender serves, in a bright hall.",
  "min": 2,
  "blurb": "A planning guide, not a rule. As a general recommendation: for up to 30 guests, one experienced bartender; for 30 to 100 guests, an experienced bartender plus support staff; for 100 or...",
  "intro": "A planning guide, not a rule. As a general recommendation: for up to 30 guests, one experienced bartender; for 30 to 100 guests, an experienced bartender plus support staff; for 100 or more, a team of bartenders, and we usually suggest a bar company that can supply the stock, glassware, ice and bar if you want them.",
  "body": [
   [
    "What pushes the number up",
    "- **A cocktail menu** takes longer per drink than beer, wine or a short list of simple mixed drinks.\n- **A peak.** Guests arriving together, or a toast, create a queue that a quiet evening does not.\n- **Short service windows.** A two-hour welcome drinks service is busier than a five-hour party.\n- **No support.** A bartender who also fetches ice and clears glasses serves fewer drinks.\n- **Layout.** Two service points halve the walking but need two people."
   ],
   [
    "The three sizes",
    "| Guests | What we recommend | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Up to 30 | One experienced bartender | A steady house party with a short cocktail list is comfortably served by one person |\n| 30 to 100 | An experienced bartender plus support staff | Support staff keep ice, glasses and restocking moving, so the bartender can keep serving |\n| 100 or more | A team of bartenders, usually through a bar company | A bar company can provide the bar, stock, glassware and ice at your request, and run the whole service |\n\nThese are planning estimates for a conversation with the bartender or bar company, and they change with your menu, arrival pattern and venue."
   ],
   [
    "How to plan it",
    "1. Choose the drinks you will offer. See [how to plan a cocktail menu](/stories/how-to-plan-a-cocktail-menu-for-a-party).\n2. Decide the busiest hour and how many guests will want a drink in it.\n3. Decide who helps with ice and glasses.\n4. Request the positions you need on your Event Card. Each position is invited and confirmed separately.\n\nFor a corporate event, see [staffing a product launch or drinks reception](https://ffstaff.uk/stories/staffing-a-product-launch-or-drinks-reception)."
   ]
  ],
